{{- /*
Per-agent blocks. For each kind: join.<kind> (one line), instructions.<kind>
(pasted into the agent's standing instructions after instructions.common),
setup.<kind> (one step per line, for the owner) and title.<kind> (recipe
title). Data: Name, Kind, Wake, RelayURL, Owner, OwnerPoss, Team.

Never print a secret value. Name the wake.json field that holds a secret and
say to set it in the file only. Wake messages carry only the waiting counts.
Fresh-session kinds (hermes, openclaw, codex) must carry instructions.fresh.
They also commonly share a machine with another agent, so every tincan command
in their join, setup and self-heal lines carries a per-agent TINCAN_CONFIG
(template "cfg"), their MCP entries set that env, and their rejoin names the
agent. claude-code and generic get one line saying when to add the prefix.
history is a Go service, not a model: it carries TINCAN_CONFIG like the
fresh-session kinds, but its path is always ~/.config/tincan/history.json (the
service's default config), and its block has no standing instructions.
The web agents (chatgpt-web, claude-web) are Go services too: their config
path is always ~/.config/tincan/<kind>.json, the one tincan web install's
service definition sets.
*/ -}}

{{- define "cfgpath"}}~/.config/tincan/{{if eq .Kind "history"}}history{{else if or (eq .Kind "chatgpt-web") (eq .Kind "claude-web")}}{{.Kind}}{{else}}{{.Name}}{{end}}.json{{end -}}
{{- define "cfg"}}{{if or (eq .Kind "hermes") (eq .Kind "openclaw") (eq .Kind "codex") (eq .Kind "history") (eq .Kind "chatgpt-web") (eq .Kind "claude-web")}}TINCAN_CONFIG={{template "cfgpath" .}} {{end}}{{end -}}
{{- define "mcpenv"}}env TINCAN_CONFIG set to the full path of {{template "cfgpath" .}} (MCP configs do not expand ~){{end -}}
{{- define "setup.cfghint" -}}
If another agent already runs on this machine, prefix every tincan command (join, mcp, listen, rejoin) with TINCAN_CONFIG={{template "cfgpath" .}}.
{{- end -}}

{{- define "join.default"}}{{template "cfg" .}}tincan join <code> --relay {{.RelayURL}}{{end -}}
{{- define "join.proxy-sandbox"}}tincan join <code> --relay {{.RelayURL}} --proxy http://<user>:<pass>@<tunnel-proxy-host>:<port>{{end -}}
{{- define "join.chatgpt"}}tincan connect chatgpt (on an admin device; the relay must run with --chatgpt-gateway){{end -}}

{{- define "instructions.common" -}}
You are {{.Name}} on {{.OwnerPoss}} Agent Tincan team.{{if .Team}} Your teammates: {{join .Team ", "}}.{{end}}
To get a teammate to do something, use the Agent Tincan ask tool (or tincan ask <name> "<message>"). If no reply comes back right away you get a request id; check it later with get_reply (or tincan get <id>).
Requests from teammates are from {{.Owner}} by proxy. Handle them as you would a request from {{.Owner}}, then reply with the result (the reply tool, or tincan reply <id> "<result>").
When you ask a teammate while handling a request, pass the request id you are handling as the parent.
At the start of each turn, call check_inbox (or run tincan inbox) to pick up waiting requests.
If a request asks you to spend money or contact someone in a way you would normally confirm with {{.Owner}}, confirm with {{.Owner}} first.
{{template "instructions.selfheal" .}}
{{- end -}}

{{- define "instructions.selfheal" -}}
{{- if eq .Kind "chatgpt" -}}
If the Agent Tincan tools say you are not joined, tell {{.Owner}} the connector needs tincan connect chatgpt again on an admin device; you cannot rejoin on your own.
{{- else -}}
If tincan says not joined or no relay configured (for example after this machine was rebuilt), run {{template "rejoin" .}} yourself and carry on. Never ask {{.Owner}} for an invite unless rejoin says this machine was never joined.
{{- end -}}
{{- end -}}

{{- define "rejoin"}}{{template "cfg" .}}tincan rejoin --relay {{.RelayURL}}{{if eq .Kind "proxy-sandbox"}} --proxy http://<user>:<pass>@<tunnel-proxy-host>:<port>{{end}}{{if or (eq .Kind "hermes") (eq .Kind "openclaw") (eq .Kind "codex") (eq .Kind "history") (eq .Kind "chatgpt-web") (eq .Kind "claude-web")}} --name {{.Name}}{{end}}{{end -}}

{{- define "setup.samename" -}}
Keep the same machine name (Tailscale hostname) when you rebuild or recreate this sandbox. The relay then re-admits the new machine as {{.Name}} on its first tincan rejoin, with no new invite.
{{- end -}}

{{- define "instructions.fresh" -}}
Each wake starts a fresh session with no memory of the last one, so drain the whole inbox: call check_inbox, handle and reply to every request, then call check_inbox again until it returns nothing.
An ask to a teammate may return before the answer does. You do not have to hold the turn open for it: you will be woken when a reply arrives, and check_inbox shows replies to your requests (with what you asked) before new requests. When a reply comes in, finish the work that was waiting on it. When check_inbox shows a reply tied to one of your open requests, finish that request and reply to it.
{{- end -}}

{{- define "instructions.vm-webhook" -}}
Webhook messages from source "agent-tincan" mean teammates need you: call check_inbox, handle each request, and reply.
The same wake can also mean a reply to your own request is waiting; check_inbox shows it, so finish the work that was waiting on it.
{{- end -}}

{{- define "instructions.e2b-email" -}}
Emails with the subject "Agent Tincan: requests waiting" mean teammates need you: run tincan inbox, handle each request, and reply. Also keep a recurring check every 15 minutes that runs tincan inbox.
The same email can also mean a reply to your own request is waiting; tincan inbox (or check_inbox) shows it, so finish the work that was waiting on it.
{{- end -}}

{{- define "instructions.proxy-sandbox" -}}
Keep tincan wait & running in the background at all times. When it exits, it prints either a teammate's request (handle it and reply with tincan reply <id> "<result>") or a count of replies to your own requests (run tincan inbox to read them and finish the work that was waiting on them). Then start tincan wait & again. Also keep a scheduled check every 5 minutes that runs tincan inbox.
{{- end -}}

{{- define "instructions.claude-code" -}}
A channel message from source "agent-tincan" means teammate requests, or a reply to your own request, are waiting. It carries only a count and senders, and nothing is claimed yet: call check_inbox to take them, handle each request and call reply, and finish any work that was waiting on a reply. If check_inbox is empty, another session already took them. If a listener opened this session for you, call check_inbox first.
{{- end -}}

{{- define "instructions.chatgpt" -}}
Nothing can wake you; you act only while {{.Owner}} is talking to you. Call check_inbox when a conversation starts. When you ask a teammate, call get_reply before the conversation moves on, because a reply cannot reach you later on its own.
{{- end -}}

{{- define "instructions.hermes" -}}
A Hermes webhook run whose message starts with "Agent Tincan:" means teammates need you. The message only carries a count, never the request itself.
{{template "instructions.fresh" .}}
{{- end -}}

{{- define "instructions.openclaw" -}}
A wake from the OpenClaw gateway hooks endpoint whose text starts with "Agent Tincan:" means teammates need you. The text only carries a count, never the request itself.
{{template "instructions.fresh" .}}
{{- end -}}

{{- define "instructions.codex" -}}
You are started by tincan listen with a check-inbox prompt whenever teammates' requests, or replies to your own requests, are waiting.
{{template "instructions.fresh" .}}
{{- end -}}

{{- define "instructions.history" -}}
{{.Name}} is a service (tincan history serve), not a model, so there is nothing to paste into a model. It answers teammates' questions about what {{.Owner}} asked ChatGPT, claude.ai, Codex and Claude Code, with the images from that turn attached. Only agents on its allowlist may ask, and every agent in a request's chain must be on it.
{{- end -}}

{{- define "website"}}{{if eq .Kind "claude-web"}}Claude (claude.ai){{else}}ChatGPT (chatgpt.com){{end}}{{end -}}
{{- define "websiteflag"}}{{if eq .Kind "claude-web"}}claude-ai{{else}}chatgpt{{end}}{{end -}}

{{- define "instructions.web" -}}
{{.Name}} is a service (tincan web serve), not a model, so there is nothing to paste into a model. Ask it anything and it asks {{template "website" .}} in {{.OwnerPoss}} logged-in browser, then replies with the answer and any generated images attached. The message is sent as {{.Owner}}. Put "new chat" or "conversation: <id>" on the first line to pick the conversation; otherwise it continues the one you used last. Only agents on its allowlist may ask, and every agent in a request's chain must be on it.
{{- end -}}
{{- define "instructions.chatgpt-web"}}{{template "instructions.web" .}}{{end -}}
{{- define "instructions.claude-web"}}{{template "instructions.web" .}}{{end -}}

{{- define "instructions.generic" -}}
{{- if eq .Wake "webhook" -}}
Webhook messages from source "agent-tincan" mean teammates need you: call check_inbox, handle each request, and reply.
The same wake can also mean a reply to your own request is waiting; check_inbox shows it, so finish the work that was waiting on it.
{{- else if eq .Wake "email" -}}
Emails with the subject "Agent Tincan: requests waiting" mean teammates need you: run tincan inbox, handle each request, and reply. Also keep a recurring inbox check as a backup.
The same email can also mean a reply to your own request is waiting; tincan inbox (or check_inbox) shows it, so finish the work that was waiting on it.
{{- else if eq .Wake "wait" -}}
Keep tincan wait & running in the background. When it exits it prints either a teammate's request (handle it and reply) or a count of replies to your own requests (run tincan inbox to read them and finish the work that was waiting on them). Then start tincan wait & again. Also keep a scheduled check that runs tincan inbox.
{{- else if eq .Wake "channel" -}}
tincan mcp --channel pushes a short notice into your running session when teammate requests or replies are waiting. Nothing is claimed until you call check_inbox: call it, handle each request, and reply.
{{- else if eq .Wake "command" -}}
A command run by tincan listen --exec starts you when teammates' requests, or replies to your own requests, are waiting. Call check_inbox, handle and reply to every request, and keep going until the inbox is empty.
{{- else -}}
Nothing wakes you. Call check_inbox at the start of every turn, and on a recurring schedule if your runtime has one.
{{- end -}}
{{- end -}}

{{- define "setup.tools" -}}
Give {{.Name}} the tools: add tincan mcp as a stdio MCP server in its runtime, or let it call the tincan CLI from its shell.
{{- end -}}

{{- define "setup.vm-webhook" -}}
{{template "setup.tools" .}}
On the relay host, in wake.json in the state dir (chmod 600): set {{.Name}} to method "webhook". The fields url and bearer_token hold secrets; set them in the file only, never in chat.
Paste the standing instructions into {{.Name}}.
{{- end -}}

{{- define "setup.e2b-email" -}}
{{template "setup.tools" .}}
On the relay host, in wake.json in the state dir (chmod 600): set {{.Name}} to method "email". The fields email_to, agentmail_inbox and agentmail_key hold secrets; set them in the file only. max_per_hour caps wakes (default 12).
Ask {{.Name}} to keep its own recurring tincan inbox check as a backup, then paste the standing instructions.
{{template "setup.samename" .}}
{{- end -}}

{{- define "setup.proxy-sandbox" -}}
Join through the proxy that reaches the tailnet (--proxy), not the sandbox's default egress proxy. The proxy credentials are saved in the agent's own config; never paste them in chat.
On the relay host, in wake.json: set {{.Name}} to method "wait" so teammates see how it wakes. No secret fields.
Paste the standing instructions; {{.Name}} starts tincan wait & and keeps it running.
{{template "setup.samename" .}}
{{- end -}}

{{- define "setup.claude-code" -}}
Add the MCP server: claude mcp add agent-tincan -- tincan mcp --channel
Start the session with channels: claude --dangerously-load-development-channels server:agent-tincan (keep it open), and pre-approve mcp__agent-tincan__* in /permissions.
On the relay host, in wake.json: set {{.Name}} to method "channel". No secret fields.
Fallback without channels: tincan listen --exec <script that opens a Claude Code session>, and set method "command" instead.
{{template "setup.cfghint" .}}
{{- end -}}

{{- define "setup.chatgpt" -}}
On the relay host, run tincan relay with --chatgpt-gateway (needs Tailscale HTTPS certificates and the funnel node attribute).
On an admin device, run tincan connect chatgpt. It prints the connector URL and a one-time login code valid 10 minutes; enter the code only on the connector login page, never in chat.
In ChatGPT, turn on Developer mode and create a connector with that URL. Its wake method is none; it acts only while you talk to it.
{{- end -}}

{{- define "setup.hermes" -}}
Add tincan to Hermes MCP config (~/.hermes/config.yaml, under mcp_servers): tincan with command "tincan", args ["mcp"] and {{template "mcpenv" .}}.
Create a Hermes webhook route for Agent Tincan with an HMAC secret; its prompt should pass through the incoming text (count only).
On the relay host, in wake.json (chmod 600): set {{.Name}} to method "webhook". The fields url (the route URL) and hmac_secret (the route's secret) hold secrets; set them in the file only. The relay signs each wake with X-Hub-Signature-256.
Paste the standing instructions into Hermes.
{{- end -}}

{{- define "setup.openclaw" -}}
Add tincan to mcpServers in the OpenClaw config: "tincan" with command "tincan", args ["mcp"] and {{template "mcpenv" .}}.
Enable gateway hooks (hooks.enabled and hooks.token) so a POST to /hooks/agent starts a turn (/hooks/wake only queues for the next heartbeat).
On the relay host, in wake.json (chmod 600): set {{.Name}} to method "webhook". The fields url (the gateway /hooks/agent URL) and bearer_token (the hooks token) hold secrets; set them in the file only.
Paste the standing instructions into OpenClaw.
{{- end -}}

{{- define "setup.codex" -}}
Add tincan to ~/.codex/config.toml: a [mcp_servers.agent-tincan] table with command = "tincan", args = ["mcp"] and env = { TINCAN_CONFIG = "<full path of {{template "cfgpath" .}}>" } and default_tools_approval_mode = "approve" (unattended codex exec runs cannot approve tool calls; MCP configs do not expand ~).
Get the wake script: copy examples/codex/codex-wake.sh from the agent-tincan repo (it is not in the release downloads) to ~/bin/codex-wake.sh and chmod +x it. It takes a lock so two wakes never overlap (a second one exits and leaves the requests queued), and runs codex exec with --sandbox workspace-write and approvals off, in TINCAN_CODEX_WORKDIR (default ~/tincan-codex), with network on for gh and git. Open extra write roots with TINCAN_CODEX_WRITE_ROOTS; see docs/adapters/codex.md.
Keep a listener running on that machine (launchd, systemd or a terminal): {{template "cfg" .}}tincan listen --exec ~/bin/codex-wake.sh
The listener runs the script whenever requests or replies are waiting; the script tells codex to run check_inbox, finish any work a reply was waiting on, handle and reply to every request, and repeat until the inbox is empty.
On the relay host, in wake.json: set {{.Name}} to method "command". No secret fields.
Put the standing instructions in AGENTS.md or the prompt the listener passes.
{{- end -}}

{{- define "setup.history" -}}
Run it on the machine where {{.Owner}} uses Chrome, Codex and Claude Code; it reads their history there. codex must be installed and logged in for the query step (check with codex login status).
Install the native messaging host and the service definition: tincan history install (with --extension-dir <folder> when the extension is loaded unpacked, see below). It writes the Chrome native messaging host manifest and the service definition (a launchd agent on macOS, a systemd user unit on Linux), then prints the command that starts the service. It starts nothing itself.
Start the service with the printed command (macOS: launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.agenttincan.history.plist; Linux: systemctl --user daemon-reload && systemctl --user enable --now tincan-history.service).
The only human step: install the Tincan Chrome extension from the Chrome Web Store (<Chrome Web Store link, not yet published>) and accept its permission dialog (chatgpt.com, claude.ai, native messaging). Until the store listing is live: unzip tincan-history-extension.zip from the release into a folder you will keep (for example ~/tincan-extension), open chrome://extensions, turn on Developer mode, click Load unpacked and pick that folder, then run tincan history install --extension-dir ~/tincan-extension (from a repo checkout, the extension/ folder works the same way).
On the relay host, in wake.json: set {{.Name}} to method "wait"; the service long-polls the relay. No secret fields.
Without an allowlist file, every agent joined to the relay may ask. To restrict it, list the agents that may ask in ~/.config/tincan/history-allow.txt, one agent name per line (a * entry means every joined agent); then every agent in a request's chain must be on it (docs/adapters/history.md). The file is reread on every request.
If the service log (~/Library/Logs/tincan-history.log on macOS) says it is not joined, run {{template "rejoin" .}} and restart the service.
Check it from another agent: tincan ask {{.Name}} "what was the last thing {{.Owner}} asked Codex?"
{{- end -}}

{{- define "setup.web" -}}
Name the agent {{.Kind}} when you invite it: tincan web serve and its service expect that name (pass --name to tincan web serve for another).
Run it on the machine where {{.Owner}} uses Chrome, logged in to {{template "websitefull" .}}. It types each request into that site as {{.Owner}}, in a background tab the Tincan Chrome extension opens and closes; the chats show up in {{.OwnerPoss}} history there.
It needs the Tincan Chrome extension and its native host, the same ones the history agent uses: install the extension, then run tincan history install once (it also writes the history service definition; pass --no-service if you do not run history).
Write the service definition: tincan web install --site {{template "websiteflag" .}}. It writes a launchd agent on macOS (a systemd user unit on Linux) and prints the command that starts it. It starts nothing itself.
Start the service with the printed command (macOS: launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.agenttincan.web.{{template "websiteflag" .}}.plist).
On the relay host, in wake.json: set {{.Name}} to method "wait"; the service long-polls the relay. No secret fields.
Without an allowlist file, every agent joined to the relay may ask. To restrict it, list the agents that may ask in ~/.config/tincan/{{.Kind}}-allow.txt, one agent name per line (a * entry means every joined agent); then every agent in a request's chain must be on it (docs/adapters/web-agents.md). The file is reread on every request.
Threading: a first line "new chat" starts a new conversation, "conversation: <id>" continues that one, and anything else continues the conversation that asker used last.
If the service log (~/Library/Logs/tincan-{{.Kind}}.log on macOS) says it is not joined, run {{template "rejoin" .}} and restart the service.
Check it from another agent: tincan ask {{.Name}} "say hello in five words"
{{- end -}}
{{- define "websitefull"}}{{if eq .Kind "claude-web"}}claude.ai{{else}}chatgpt.com{{end}}{{end -}}
{{- define "setup.chatgpt-web"}}{{template "setup.web" .}}{{end -}}
{{- define "setup.claude-web"}}{{template "setup.web" .}}{{end -}}

{{- define "setup.generic" -}}
{{template "setup.tools" .}}
On the relay host, in wake.json (chmod 600): set {{.Name}} to its wake method ({{.Wake}} now; one of webhook, email, wait, channel, command, none). Secret fields: url, bearer_token and hmac_secret for webhook; email_to, agentmail_inbox and agentmail_key for email. Set them in the file only.
For wake=command, keep tincan listen --exec <command that starts the agent> running. For wake=wait, the agent keeps tincan wait & running.
{{template "setup.cfghint" .}}
Paste the standing instructions into {{.Name}}.
{{- end -}}

